# Cline rules for TASAMI

Read `CLAUDE.md` first — it is the full working brief. This file is the short
version an agent must not violate, plus the things that have actually broken
this repo before.

## Before you change anything

- `npm run typecheck` and `npm run build` are the quality gates. There is **no
  lint script**, and you must not add one: the repo has no eslint config and
  Next 16 removed `next lint`.
- `npm test` needs a real PostgreSQL database and truncates tables between
  cases. Point `DATABASE_URL` at a scratch database, **never** a working one.

## Never do these

- **Never bypass the approval gate.** An agent may only ever create a `PENDING`
  approval. `dispatchApproval` is the sole path to `SENT` and requires an
  already-`APPROVED` row. A failure must never produce a false `SENT`.
- **Never invent data.** No fabricated dashboard figures, no guessed contact
  details for a real business, no fixture standing in for a live integration. A
  page with no data source says so; a missing credential fails closed with 503.
- **Never write a secret into the repo.** No `.env`, no keys in code, comments,
  commits or logs. Configuration is server-side only.
- **Never delete `prisma/migrations/`.** With an empty migrations directory
  `prisma migrate deploy` prints "No migration found" and exits 0 — a green
  deploy against an unmigrated database.
- **Never upgrade Prisma past 6.x** on an incidental change. Prisma 7 drops
  `datasource.url` from schema.prisma for a driver adapter; that is its own
  branch.

## Traps that have cost real time here

- **`??` on an environment variable is a bug.** It falls back only on
  `null`/`undefined`, so a blank value in a hosting dashboard passes straight
  through — this took down a production build via `new URL('')`. Use `||` when
  reading `process.env` directly.
- **Every query is scoped by `userId`.** `updateMany`/`deleteMany` with
  `{ id, userId }`, `findFirst` rather than `findUnique`. A guessed id must read
  as not-found. This repo has had real IDOR bugs.
- **`updateMany` with empty data counts 0**, which reads as not-found even when
  the row exists and is owned. Check ownership explicitly when a PATCH body may
  be empty.
- **A new agent type needs an entry in `MICRO_BUDGET_USD`**
  (`src/lib/ai/policies.ts`). Without one, `evaluatePolicy` blocks it as
  "Unknown agent" — provisioned, listed in the UI, and dead on click.
- **Money is integer minor units.** The client sends a plan code and an interval
  and nothing else. No feature code branches on a plan code —
  `src/lib/billing/entitlements.ts` is the single authority.
- **Northflank secret PATCH replaces `secrets.variables`, it does not merge.**
  Sending one key wipes the rest. Read the whole group, merge, send it whole,
  then verify the count did not drop. Five secrets were lost here this way.

## Style

Comments explain *why*, especially where the code looks odd — the ordering
constraints, the atomic statements, the deliberate refusals. Do not add comments
that restate the line beneath them.

Tests assert against real database behaviour. External services are substituted
at the network boundary (`__setProvider`, `__setDiscoveryProvider`), never by
mocking the logic under test. Derive expectations from source data
(`AGENT_DEFAULTS.length`) rather than hardcoding counts.

## Ask before

Changing billing, pricing, the PayPal integration, the database schema, or
`PAYPAL_ENVIRONMENT`. These carry money or migration risk and are the owner's
call.
